\chapter{Tests in C\#}\label{ch:win-tests}

The tests of the core are ported along with the core. Their purpose is the one stated in
Chapter~\ref{ch:tests}: each test pins down a property the rest of the program depends on. For the
port there is a second purpose. Since the algorithms are meant to be identical on both systems, a
test that passes in Swift and in C\# with the same inputs, expected values and tolerances is
evidence that the transcription is faithful. So every XCTest test has a C\# twin with the same
numbers. The port also adds tests of its own, for the two pieces that are new: the FFT and the CPU
filters that replace Core Image.

\section{The test project}

The tests use xUnit, the most common test framework for .NET. The project references the test SDK
(which lets [[dotnet test]] find and run the tests), xUnit itself, and its runner for Visual Studio
and the command line. It references the core project, not the app, so the tests run without WPF.
The [[Using]] item imports the [[Xunit]] namespace into every file, the way [[import XCTest]] does
in Swift.

The Swift tests used [[@testable import]] to reach internal members. The C\# counterpart is on the
other side: the core's project file grants [[InternalsVisibleTo]] to this test assembly
(Chapter~\ref{ch:win-core}), which lets the tests call the internal [[Fft]] class and the filter
kernels.

<<Windows/LineScope.Core.Tests/LineScope.Core.Tests.csproj>>=
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net9.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.12.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="2.8.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\LineScope.Core\LineScope.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Using Include="Xunit" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
@

\section{The test file}

The file keeps the structure of the Swift one: a class per area, a method per property. In xUnit a
test is a public method marked [[[Fact]]], and the class needs no base class. The method names drop
Swift's [[test]] prefix, which XCTest needed to discover them. A single test is run with a filter on
its full name:
\begin{lstlisting}[language={}]
dotnet test LineScope.Core.Tests --filter "FullyQualifiedName~SpectrumTests.SinePeak"
\end{lstlisting}

<<Windows/LineScope.Core.Tests/LineScopeCoreTests.cs>>=
using LineScope.Core;

namespace LineScope.Core.Tests;

<<Win Tests: helpers>>

<<Win Tests: spectrum>>

<<Win Tests: resampling>>

<<Win Tests: color conversions>>

<<Win Tests: line sampler>>

<<Win Tests: bitmap bridge>>

<<Win Tests: filters>>

<<Win Tests: test patterns>>
@

The gradient fixture is the one of Chapter~\ref{ch:tests}: pixel $(x,y)$ is
$(x/W,\ y/H,\ \frac14)$, opaque. xUnit has no counterpart of XCTest's
[[XCTAssertEqual(_:_:accuracy:)]], so a small helper provides it: [[Near]] fails unless the two
values differ by at most the accuracy, and the optional label says which case failed when a test
loops over methods or bins.

<<Win Tests: helpers>>=
internal static class Helpers
{
    public static PixelBuffer Gradient(int width, int height)
    {
        var buf = new PixelBuffer(width, height);
        for (int y = 0; y < height; y++)
            for (int x = 0; x < width; x++)
                buf[x, y] = new Rgba((float)x / width, (float)y / height, 0.25f, 1);
        return buf;
    }

    public static void Near(double expected, double actual, double accuracy, string? label = null) =>
        Assert.True(Math.Abs(expected - actual) <= accuracy,
            $"{label} expected {expected} ± {accuracy}, got {actual}");
}
@

\section{Spectrum}

The first two tests are the Swift ones: a sine of $k=16$ periods in $n=256$ samples must put an
amplitude of $1$ in bin $16$, at $16/256$ cycles per pixel, with the last bin at Nyquist; and a
constant $0.3$ with the mean kept must give amplitude $0.3$ in bin $0$, with $100$ samples padded
to an FFT of $128$. They check the normalization and the frequency axis, which the port keeps from
vDSP's conventions, and they pass unchanged.

<<Win Tests: spectrum>>=
public class SpectrumTests
{
    [Fact]
    public void SinePeak()
    {
        const int n = 256, k = 16;
        var values = Enumerable.Range(0, n).Select(i => (float)Math.Sin(2 * Math.PI * k * i / n)).ToArray();
        var s = SpectrumAnalyzer.Compute(values, 1, WindowFunction.None, removeMean: true);
        Assert.Equal(256, s.FftSize);
        Assert.Equal(k, s.PeakIndex);
        Helpers.Near(1, s.Amplitudes[k], 1e-3);
        Helpers.Near((double)k / n, s.CyclesPerPixel[k], 1e-9);
        Helpers.Near(0.5, s.CyclesPerPixel[^1], 1e-9);
    }

    [Fact]
    public void DCAmplitude()
    {
        var s = SpectrumAnalyzer.Compute(Enumerable.Repeat(0.3f, 100).ToArray(), 1, WindowFunction.None, removeMean: false);
        Assert.Equal(128, s.FftSize);
        Helpers.Near(0.3, s.Amplitudes[0], 1e-4);
    }

    <<Win Tests: the FFT against a naive DFT>>
}
@

These tests would not catch every mistake in a hand-written FFT. A pure sine concentrates its
energy in one bin, and several classic errors, such as a wrong twiddle factor in a later stage,
leave a sine's peak in the right place while scrambling the other bins. The macOS version didn't
need more, since vDSP is a trusted library; the port's own FFT (Chapter~\ref{ch:win-core}) does.

The new test compares the FFT with the \emph{definition} of the discrete Fourier transform,
\[
  X_k = \sum_{j=0}^{N-1} x_j\, e^{-2\pi i\, jk/N}, \qquad k = 0, \dots, N-1,
\]
computed directly, in $O(N^2)$ operations, for $N=64$ random real samples. The random generator is
seeded, so the test is repeatable. Every bin must agree to $10^{-9}$. In double precision the
rounding error of both computations is around $10^{-14}$ for sums of this size, so the tolerance is
generous for a correct FFT and far too tight for a wrong one, whose errors are of the order of the
values themselves. The inputs are real because the analyzer only ever transforms real profiles.

<<Win Tests: the FFT against a naive DFT>>=
[Fact]
public void FftMatchesNaiveDft()
{
    var rng = new Random(3);
    var x = Enumerable.Range(0, 64).Select(_ => new System.Numerics.Complex(rng.NextDouble(), 0)).ToArray();
    var fast = (System.Numerics.Complex[])x.Clone();
    Fft.Forward(fast);
    for (int k = 0; k < x.Length; k++)
    {
        System.Numerics.Complex sum = 0;
        for (int j = 0; j < x.Length; j++)
            sum += x[j] * System.Numerics.Complex.FromPolarCoordinates(1, -2 * Math.PI * j * k / x.Length);
        Helpers.Near(0, (sum - fast[k]).Magnitude, 1e-9, $"bin {k}");
    }
}
@

\section{Resampling}

The four resampling tests are transcriptions of the Swift ones, and Chapter~\ref{ch:tests} derives
their expected values: the interpolating kernels are $1$ at $0$ and $0$ at the other integers,
Mitchell-Netravali gives $k(0)=\frac89$ and $k(1)=\frac1{18}$, Catmull-Rom gives
$k(\frac12)=\frac9{16}$, and the sinc is truncated at its radius; every method but Mitchell is the
identity at scale $1$; the area method averages exact pairs at scale $\frac12$; and a constant image
stays constant at every scale. The kernel function is internal, as in Swift.

<<Win Tests: resampling>>=
public class ResamplingTests
{
    <<Win Tests: kernel shapes>>

    <<Win Tests: identity at scale one>>

    <<Win Tests: area average halves>>

    <<Win Tests: constants stay constant>>
}
@

<<Win Tests: kernel shapes>>=
[Fact]
public void KernelShapes()
{
    // Interpolating kernels are 1 at 0 and 0 at nonzero integers.
    foreach (var method in new[] { ResampleMethod.Tent, ResampleMethod.CatmullRom, ResampleMethod.Lanczos3, ResampleMethod.Sinc })
    {
        Helpers.Near(1, Resampler.Kernel(method, 0), 1e-12, method.ToString());
        for (int k = 1; k <= 3; k++)
            Helpers.Near(0, Resampler.Kernel(method, k), 1e-12, $"{method} at {k}");
    }
    // Mitchell-Netravali (B = C = 1/3) is approximating: k(0) = 8/9, k(1) = 1/18.
    Helpers.Near(8.0 / 9, Resampler.Kernel(ResampleMethod.Mitchell, 0), 1e-12);
    Helpers.Near(1.0 / 18, Resampler.Kernel(ResampleMethod.Mitchell, 1), 1e-12);
    Helpers.Near(0, Resampler.Kernel(ResampleMethod.Mitchell, 2), 1e-12);
    Helpers.Near(0.5625, Resampler.Kernel(ResampleMethod.CatmullRom, 0.5), 1e-12);
    // Sinc is truncated at its radius.
    Assert.Equal(0, Resampler.Kernel(ResampleMethod.Sinc, Resampler.SincRadius + 0.5));
}
@

[[ResampleMethodInfo.All]] stands in for Swift's [[ResampleMethod.allCases]]: a C\# enumeration has
no built-in list of its cases, so the core provides one.

<<Win Tests: identity at scale one>>=
[Fact]
public void IdentityAtScaleOne()
{
    var src = Helpers.Gradient(17, 9);
    // Mitchell is approximating (slightly smoothing), so it is not an identity at ×1.
    foreach (var method in ResampleMethodInfo.All.Where(m => m != ResampleMethod.Mitchell))
    {
        var output = Resampler.Resample(src, 1, method);
        Assert.Equal(17, output.Width);
        Assert.Equal(9, output.Height);
        for (int i = 0; i < src.Data.Length; i++)
            Helpers.Near(src.Data[i], output.Data[i], 1e-5, method.ToString());
    }
}
@

<<Win Tests: area average halves>>=
[Fact]
public void AreaHalvesAverages()
{
    var src = new PixelBuffer(4, 1);
    float[] values = [0, 1, 0.2f, 0.6f];
    for (int x = 0; x < values.Length; x++) src[x, 0] = new Rgba(values[x], values[x], values[x]);
    var output = Resampler.Resample(src, 0.5, ResampleMethod.Area);
    Assert.Equal(2, output.Width);
    Helpers.Near(0.5, output[0, 0].R, 1e-5);
    Helpers.Near(0.4, output[1, 0].R, 1e-5);
}
@

<<Win Tests: constants stay constant>>=
[Fact]
public void ConstantImageStaysConstant()
{
    var src = new PixelBuffer(13, 7, new Rgba(0.4f, 0.5f, 0.6f));
    foreach (var method in ResampleMethodInfo.All)
        foreach (double scale in new[] { 0.3, 0.5, 2.0, 3.7 })
        {
            var output = Resampler.Resample(src, scale, method);
            for (int i = 0; i < output.Data.Length; i += 4)
                Helpers.Near(0.4, output.Data[i], 1e-4, $"{method} {scale}");
        }
}
@

\section{Color conversions and line sampling}

The color tests are the same round trips as in Swift, with the same colors, and the same absolute
check that pure blue has hue $240^\circ$. The line sampler tests are the same too: $201$ and $101$
samples on a $200$-pixel image and its half-size copy, and a vertical line at $u=0.505$ that reads
exactly the red of pixel column $50$. The C\# index-from-end operator [[^1]] replaces Swift's
[[last!]].

<<Win Tests: color conversions>>=
public class ColorTests
{
    [Fact]
    public void HslRoundTrip()
    {
        foreach (var c in new[] { new Rgba(0.2f, 0.7f, 0.4f), new Rgba(1, 0, 0), new Rgba(0.1f, 0.1f, 0.9f), new Rgba(0.5f, 0.5f, 0.5f) })
        {
            var hsl = ColorConversion.Hsl(c);
            var back = ColorConversion.FromHsl(hsl.H, hsl.S, hsl.L);
            Helpers.Near(c.R, back.R, 1e-5);
            Helpers.Near(c.G, back.G, 1e-5);
            Helpers.Near(c.B, back.B, 1e-5);
        }
        Helpers.Near(240, ColorConversion.Hsl(new Rgba(0, 0, 1)).H, 1e-4);
    }

    [Fact]
    public void CmykRoundTrip()
    {
        foreach (var c in new[] { new Rgba(0.2f, 0.7f, 0.4f), new Rgba(0, 0, 0), new Rgba(1, 1, 1) })
        {
            var v = ColorConversion.Cmyk(c);
            var back = ColorConversion.FromCmyk(v.C, v.M, v.Y, v.K);
            Helpers.Near(c.R, back.R, 1e-5);
            Helpers.Near(c.G, back.G, 1e-5);
            Helpers.Near(c.B, back.B, 1e-5);
        }
    }
}
@

<<Win Tests: line sampler>>=
public class LineSamplerTests
{
    [Fact]
    public void SampleCountScalesWithResample()
    {
        var src = Helpers.Gradient(200, 100);
        var half = Resampler.Resample(src, 0.5, ResampleMethod.Tent);
        var a = LineSampler.Sample(src, LineSegment.Initial);
        var b = LineSampler.Sample(half, LineSegment.Initial);
        Assert.Equal(201, a.Colors.Length);
        Assert.Equal(101, b.Colors.Length);
        Helpers.Near(1, a.Spacing, 1e-9);
    }

    [Fact]
    public void SamplesFollowContent()
    {
        var src = Helpers.Gradient(100, 100);
        var line = new LineSegment(new NormalizedPoint(0.505, 0.1), new NormalizedPoint(0.505, 0.9));
        var s = LineSampler.Sample(src, line);
        // Vertical line through pixel column 50: red is constant, green increases.
        Helpers.Near(0.5, s.Colors[0].R, 1e-4);
        Helpers.Near(0.5, s.Colors[^1].R, 1e-4);
        Assert.True(s.Colors[0].G < s.Colors[^1].G);
    }
}
@

\section{The bitmap bridge}

On macOS the bridge to the screen was a [[CGImage]]; here it is an array of 8-bit premultiplied
BGRA bytes, the layout of WPF's [[Pbgra32]] format (Chapter~\ref{ch:win-core}). The round-trip test
is the Swift one: converting to bytes and back may lose one quantization step, $\frac1{255}$, and
must keep the size and the row order.

A second test checks what the round trip can't see: the \emph{order} of the bytes. If red and blue
were swapped in both directions, the round trip would still pass, and every image would show with
its colors exchanged. So one orange pixel, $(1, 0.5, 0)$ opaque, must become the bytes blue, green,
red, alpha $= (0, 128, 255, 255)$. The $128$ also checks the rounding: $0.5 \cdot 255 = 127.5$ rounds
up.

<<Win Tests: bitmap bridge>>=
public class BitmapBridgeTests
{
    [Fact]
    public void Bgra8RoundTrip()
    {
        var src = Helpers.Gradient(32, 16);
        var bytes = src.ToPremultipliedBgra8();
        var back = PixelBuffer.FromPremultipliedBgra8(32, 16, bytes);
        Assert.Equal(32, back.Width);
        Assert.Equal(16, back.Height);
        for (int i = 0; i < src.Data.Length; i++)
            Helpers.Near(src.Data[i], back.Data[i], 1.0 / 255 + 1e-4);
    }

    [Fact]
    public void Bgra8ChannelOrder()
    {
        var src = new PixelBuffer(1, 1, new Rgba(1, 0.5f, 0, 1));
        var bytes = src.ToPremultipliedBgra8();
        Assert.Equal(new byte[] { 0, 128, 255, 255 }, bytes);
    }
}
@

\section{Filters}

The filter tests grow the most, because the port reimplements the five Core Image filters on the
CPU. On macOS there was little to test there: Core Image is Apple's, and the tests only had to show
that LineScope called it correctly. Here the filters are LineScope's own code, so they get tests of
their own.

<<Win Tests: filters>>=
public class FilterTests
{
    <<Win Tests: every filter>>

    <<Win Tests: filters keep the size>>

    <<Win Tests: smoothing filters keep constants>>

    <<Win Tests: kernels are normalized>>

    <<Win Tests: the median and the noise filter>>
}
@

The tests draw on one list of filters, one of each kind, with moderate parameters.

<<Win Tests: every filter>>=
private static readonly FilterKind[] AllFilters =
[
    new FilterKind.GaussianBlur(2), new FilterKind.BoxBlur(2), new FilterKind.Median(),
    new FilterKind.UnsharpMask(2, 0.5), new FilterKind.NoiseReduction(0.02, 0.4), new FilterKind.Sobel(),
    new FilterKind.Laplacian(), new FilterKind.Emboss(), new FilterKind.Grayscale(), new FilterKind.Invert(),
];
@

The first test is the Swift one, extended to every filter: the size must be kept, since the
normalized line relies on it, and a blurred gradient must still increase from left to right. It also
checks that every output stays in $[0,1]$. That matters most for the Sobel, Laplacian and emboss
kernels, whose sums can leave the range, and for the unsharp mask, which overshoots at edges by
design; both must be brought back into range before the result is stored.

<<Win Tests: filters keep the size>>=
[Fact]
public void FiltersKeepSize()
{
    var src = Helpers.Gradient(24, 12);
    foreach (var f in AllFilters)
    {
        var output = Filters.Apply(f, src);
        Assert.Equal(24, output.Width);
        Assert.Equal(12, output.Height);
        Assert.All(output.Data, v => Assert.InRange(v, 0f, 1f));
    }
    var blurred = Filters.Apply(new FilterKind.GaussianBlur(1), src);
    // Horizontal gradient stays roughly monotone and in range after blur.
    Assert.True(blurred[2, 6].R < blurred[20, 6].R);
}
@

A smoothing filter is a weighted average whose weights sum to one, so it must leave a constant image
unchanged; so must the unsharp mask, which adds a multiple of $\mathit{src}-\mathit{blur}$, zero
for a constant, and the noise reduction, which averages equal neighbors. The test checks every pixel,
the borders included. That tests the edge handling: the filters clamp coordinates at the border,
so the kernel always sees real pixels, and a wrong clamp shows at once as a darker or lighter frame.
Red and blue ($0.3$ and $0.9$) are checked, which would also expose a channel mix-up.

<<Win Tests: smoothing filters keep constants>>=
[Fact]
public void SmoothingFiltersKeepConstantImages()
{
    var src = new PixelBuffer(9, 7, new Rgba(0.3f, 0.6f, 0.9f));
    foreach (var f in AllFilters.Where(f => f is FilterKind.GaussianBlur or FilterKind.BoxBlur or FilterKind.Median
                 or FilterKind.UnsharpMask or FilterKind.NoiseReduction))
    {
        var output = Filters.Apply(f, src);
        for (int i = 0; i < output.Data.Length; i += 4)
        {
            Helpers.Near(0.3, output.Data[i], 1e-5, f.DisplayName);
            Helpers.Near(0.9, output.Data[i + 2], 1e-5, f.DisplayName);
        }
    }
}
@

The Gaussian and box kernels are tested directly. For every radius, including $0$ and the fractional
$0.4$, the weights must sum to one. Two exact shapes pin the box convention: the box covers
$[-(r+\frac12),\ r+\frac12]$, so radius $0$ is the identity, a single weight $1$, and radius $1$ is a
three-pixel box.

<<Win Tests: kernels are normalized>>=
[Fact]
public void KernelsAreNormalized()
{
    foreach (double r in new[] { 0.0, 0.4, 1, 2.5, 10 })
    {
        Helpers.Near(1, Filters.GaussianKernel(r).Sum(), 1e-5, $"gaussian {r}");
        Helpers.Near(1, Filters.BoxKernel(r).Sum(), 1e-5, $"box {r}");
    }
    Assert.Equal(new float[] { 1 }, Filters.BoxKernel(0));
    Assert.Equal(3, Filters.BoxKernel(1).Length);
}
@

The last two tests check the defining behavior of the two nonlinear filters. A $3\times3$ median
removes an isolated impulse completely: among the nine values in the window only one is the bright
pixel, and the median is the background, exactly. The noise reduction averages a neighbor only when
its luminance differs from the center by less than the noise level. A bump of $0.005$ under a level
of $0.02$ is therefore averaged away, to within $0.002$ of the background, and, not being an edge,
is not sharpened back.

<<Win Tests: the median and the noise filter>>=
[Fact]
public void MedianRemovesAnImpulse()
{
    var src = new PixelBuffer(5, 5, new Rgba(0.2f, 0.2f, 0.2f));
    src[2, 2] = new Rgba(1, 1, 1);
    var output = Filters.Apply(new FilterKind.Median(), src);
    Helpers.Near(0.2, output[2, 2].R, 1e-6);
}

[Fact]
public void NoiseReductionSmoothsSmallVariations()
{
    var src = new PixelBuffer(5, 5, new Rgba(0.5f, 0.5f, 0.5f));
    src[2, 2] = new Rgba(0.505f, 0.505f, 0.505f);
    var output = Filters.Apply(new FilterKind.NoiseReduction(0.02, 0.4), src);
    Assert.True(Math.Abs(output[2, 2].R - 0.5) < 0.002);
}
@

\section{Test patterns}

The first three pattern tests are the Swift ones: every pattern has the requested size and stays in
$[0,1]$; a sine grating of $0.125$ cycles per pixel, sampled along the default line, has its
spectral peak within one bin of $0.125$, which tests generator, sampler and analyzer together; and
the checkerboard and the seeded noise are exact and repeatable. Comparing two noise buffers with
[[Assert.Equal]] works because [[PixelBuffer]] implements value equality over its pixels
(Chapter~\ref{ch:win-core}), while the class itself is a reference type.

<<Win Tests: test patterns>>=
public class TestPatternTests
{
    [Fact]
    public void AllPatternsGenerateRequestedSize()
    {
        foreach (var kind in TestPatternKindInfo.All)
        {
            var buf = TestPatternGenerator.Make(new TestPatternSpec(kind, 64, 48));
            Assert.Equal(64, buf.Width);
            Assert.Equal(48, buf.Height);
            Assert.All(buf.Data, v => Assert.InRange(v, 0f, 1f));
        }
    }

    [Fact]
    public void SineGratingPeaksAtItsFrequency()
    {
        var spec = new TestPatternSpec(TestPatternKind.SineGrating, 256, 8, 0.125);
        var samples = LineSampler.Sample(TestPatternGenerator.Make(spec), LineSegment.Initial);
        var values = samples.Colors.Select(c => c.R).ToArray();
        var s = SpectrumAnalyzer.Compute(values, samples.Spacing, WindowFunction.Hann, removeMean: true);
        int peak = Assert.NotNull(s.PeakIndex);
        Helpers.Near(0.125, s.CyclesPerPixel[peak], 1.0 / s.FftSize + 1e-9);
    }

    [Fact]
    public void CheckerboardAndNoiseDeterminism()
    {
        var board = TestPatternGenerator.Make(new TestPatternSpec(TestPatternKind.Checkerboard, 8, 8, 2));
        Assert.Equal(1, board[0, 0].R);
        Assert.Equal(0, board[2, 0].R);
        Assert.Equal(1, board[2, 2].R);
        var a = TestPatternGenerator.Make(new TestPatternSpec(TestPatternKind.WhiteNoise, 16, 16, 7));
        var b = TestPatternGenerator.Make(new TestPatternSpec(TestPatternKind.WhiteNoise, 16, 16, 7));
        Assert.Equal(a, b);
    }

    <<Win Tests: SplitMix64 reference values>>
}
@

The repeatability test only shows that a seed gives the same noise twice on the \emph{same} system.
The port promises more: the same seed must give the same noise on both systems, so that a noise
pattern on Windows is the pattern on the Mac. That holds only if the C\# SplitMix64 is bit for bit
the algorithm of Chapter~\ref{ch:patterns}, with its unsigned 64-bit wrap-around arithmetic, which
C\# has to be told about with [[ulong]] and unchecked operations. The last test compares the first two
outputs for seed $0$ with the values of Vigna's reference implementation.

<<Win Tests: SplitMix64 reference values>>=
[Fact]
public void SplitMixMatchesReferenceSequence()
{
    // Reference values of SplitMix64 seeded with 0 (Vigna's reference implementation).
    var rng = new SplitMix64(0);
    Assert.Equal(0xE220A8397B1DCDAFUL, rng.Next());
    Assert.Equal(0x6E789E6AA1B965F4UL, rng.Next());
}
@
